N2  -  Objective: To analyze the clinical efficacy and safety of total thoracoscopic lobectomy in the treatment of early non‐small cell lung cancer. Methods: A total of 120 patients with early stage non‐small cell lung cancer admitted to the Department of respiratory medicine from April 2016 to May 2019 were enrolled in the control group and the observation group according to the principle of randomized control. Thoracic lobectomy, total thoracoscopic lobectomy was performed in the observation group, and hospitalization time, chest drainage time, operation time, intraoperative blood loss, contrast lymph node dissection, and postoperative complication rate were collected. The data was compared statistically. Results: Compared with the control group, the observation group significantly shortened the hospitalization time, and the operation time was shortened. The intraoperative blood loss was significantly reduced. The results were statistically significant (P < 0.05). The chest drainage time of the two groups was There was no significant difference after statistical analysis, and the difference was not statistically significant (P > 0.05). The data of lymphatic dissection, perioperative mortality, and drainage volume in the observation group and the control group were statistically analyzed and found to be no significant difference. The difference was not statistically significant (P > 0.05). The incidence of complications in the observation group and the control group were 3.33% and 25.00%, respectively. Statistically, statistically significant differences were found and the incidence of complications in the observation group was significantly reduced. Conclusions: Total thoracoscopic lobectomy in the treatment of early stage non‐small cell lung cancer can significantly improve the operation of patients, help postoperative recovery, and reduce the incidence of complications, which is worthy of clinical promotion.
